{"lines": ["Now, the sound everyone remembers. The Bloop.", "Summer, 1997. An array of autonomous hydrophones in the equatorial Pacific records an ultra-low-frequency, high-amplitude sound. It rises in frequency for about a minute. It is heard on several sensors more than five thousand kilometres apart. Triangulation puts it near fifty degrees south, one hundred degrees west, in the South Pacific.", "Christopher Fox, of NOAA, first told CNN in 2001 that he believed it was ice calving in Antarctica. Then in 2002 he told New Scientist that the sound resembled the profile of a living creature, but would be far more powerful than the calls made by any animal on Earth. The first half of that sentence travelled much further than the second.", "The name stuck, and so did the monster.", "Rule three. A nickname is not a diagnosis. A shape that reminds you of an animal does not make it one."], "words": [["Now,", 0.0, 0.2], ["the", 0.2, 0.48], ["sound", 0.48, 0.74], ["everyone", 0.74, 1.2], ["remembers.", 1.2, 1.86], ["The", 1.86, 2.28], ["Bloop.", 2.28, 2.58], ["Summer,", 3.28, 3.64], ["1997.", 3.64, 4.56], ["An", 5.24, 5.42], ["array", 5.42, 5.66], ["of", 5.66, 5.86], ["autonomous", 5.86, 6.34], ["hydrophones", 6.34, 7.06], ["in", 7.06, 7.26], ["the", 7.26, 7.38], ["equatorial", 7.38, 7.86], ["Pacific", 7.86, 8.26], ["records", 8.26, 8.76], ["an", 8.76, 9.467], ["ultra-low-frequency,", 9.467, 10.173], ["high-amplitude", 10.173, 10.88], ["sound.", 10.88, 11.26], ["It", 11.98, 12.03], ["rises", 12.03, 12.3], ["in", 12.3, 12.58], ["frequency", 12.58, 12.84], ["for", 12.84, 13.32], ["about", 13.32, 13.48], ["a", 13.48, 13.66], ["minute.", 13.66, 13.9], ["It", 14.36, 14.44], ["is", 14.44, 14.54], ["heard", 14.54, 14.76], ["on", 14.76, 14.94], ["several", 14.94, 15.18], ["sensors", 15.18, 15.74], ["more", 15.74, 16.08], ["than", 16.08, 16.24], ["five", 16.24, 16.58], ["thousand", 16.58, 16.92], ["kilometres", 16.92, 17.26], ["apart.", 17.26, 17.92], ["Triangulation", 18.58, 19.06]